1
00:01:47,898 --> 00:01:48,941
Clear.

2
00:02:15,635 --> 00:02:19,680
- "Stand down. Exercise complete."
- Damn. We'll get you guys next time.

3
00:02:20,181 --> 00:02:22,266
- We won?
- Yes.

4
00:02:30,983 --> 00:02:33,694
- F.B.I.'s having a good showing this year.
- Thank you.

5
00:02:35,071 --> 00:02:36,697
- Colonel?
- Yes?

6
00:02:37,990 --> 00:02:39,533
I believe you have
something for me.

7
00:02:48,668 --> 00:02:52,213
- Week's not over yet.
- Bring it on.

8
00:03:06,018 --> 00:03:09,355
This idea you have about quitting
were you ever gonna bring it up again?

9
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:10,898
Well, it's just a thought.

10
00:03:11,816 --> 00:03:13,859
For the record, I enjoy what we do.

11
00:03:13,984 --> 00:03:16,821
Well, you might enjoy Paris
or learning pedal steel guitar.

12
00:03:17,279 --> 00:03:21,575
- Pedal steel guitar?
- The world is infinite in its possibilities.

13
00:03:22,785 --> 00:03:25,204
It's not gonna be as easy
to walk away as you think.

14
00:03:25,621 --> 00:03:26,664
Why do you say that?

15
00:03:26,789 --> 00:03:29,959
You enjoy the mental stimulation
far more than you let on.

16
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:33,045
- Agent Lisbon.
- Colonel Raymond.

17
00:03:33,504 --> 00:03:36,966
We met at a seminar...
"Tactical Solutions to Urban Terrorism."

18
00:03:37,508 --> 00:03:38,801
Colonel Raymond,
this is Patrick Jane.

19
00:03:38,926 --> 00:03:40,553
He works as a consultant
with the team.

20
00:03:43,305 --> 00:03:46,016
That was some fine work back there
clearing those rooms just now.

21
00:03:46,142 --> 00:03:48,227
If you ever decide you're
tired of the Bureau

22
00:03:48,602 --> 00:03:51,147
- I can have a space for you.
- That's flattering. Thank you.

23
00:03:52,148 --> 00:03:53,232
Now if you'll excuse me

24
00:03:53,357 --> 00:03:55,776
I need to talk to a couple men
about ambush tactics.

25
00:03:55,901 --> 00:03:57,194
Yeah.

26
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:05,119
Did... did you ever have
a fling with that guy?

27
00:04:05,536 --> 00:04:08,164
- What?
- Of course not. Sorry.

28
00:04:09,123 --> 00:04:10,166
It's just strange.

29
00:04:10,624 --> 00:04:13,210
Strange that I didn't have
a fling with Colonel Raymond?

30
00:04:14,628 --> 00:04:16,172
Yeah, there's something
going on there.

31
00:04:19,091 --> 00:04:20,634
- You don't know?
- Know what?

32
00:04:20,968 --> 00:04:22,595
His wife was murdered
eight months ago.

33
00:04:23,846 --> 00:04:25,556
- Really?
- It was all over the news.

34
00:04:27,224 --> 00:04:28,350
Did they catch anyone?

35
00:04:29,018 --> 00:04:30,728
Yeah, they arrested somebody right after.

36
00:04:31,854 --> 00:04:32,897
I need a shower.

37
00:04:38,819 --> 00:04:39,987
Eight months ago, huh?

38
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:02,883
The murder of Colonel Raymond's
wife is closed.

39
00:05:05,552 --> 00:05:07,554
So you're not interested
in hearing my theory?

40
00:05:11,558 --> 00:05:13,936
Okay. What's your theory?

41
00:05:14,061 --> 00:05:16,522
Well, I don't have one yet
but that's why we need to look into it.

42
00:05:17,356 --> 00:05:18,732
{\an8}Excuse me. Sorry.

43
00:05:19,066 --> 00:05:20,275
{\an8}Cho needs you to sign this.

44
00:05:22,444 --> 00:05:23,737
{\an8}So you don't have anything?

45
00:05:24,279 --> 00:05:25,989
No. At this point,
I'm just curious.

46
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:27,616
Curiosity killed the cat.

47
00:05:33,163 --> 00:05:34,331
{\an8}Also cured polio.

48
00:05:35,249 --> 00:05:38,502
{\an8}Aaron Raymond is not just
a colonel in the Marines.

49
00:05:38,836 --> 00:05:40,420
{\an8}He's a decorated war hero.

50
00:05:40,796 --> 00:05:42,548
{\an8}And they've already arrested
a man for the murder.

51
00:05:42,673 --> 00:05:45,551
{\an8}So what makes you think that man
didn't commit the crime?

52
00:05:45,676 --> 00:05:48,345
{\an8}Well, I'm not sure that he didn't.
I'm just saying, there is...

53
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:50,889
{\an8}there is something off about
this Colonel Raymond.

54
00:05:56,270 --> 00:05:57,604
{\an8}Lisbon, what do you think?

55
00:05:58,564 --> 00:06:01,275
{\an8}If Jane says it's worth looking into
then it's worth looking into.

56
00:06:01,483 --> 00:06:02,985
{\an8}You know to trust his instincts.

57
00:06:05,362 --> 00:06:08,031
{\an8}No interrogations, no searches.

58
00:06:08,991 --> 00:06:11,243
{\an8}If you wanna talk to anyone,
use a cover story.

59
00:06:11,702 --> 00:06:14,329
{\an8}A civil rights case for the man
who was arrested maybe.

60
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:17,875
{\an8}Raymond is not to learn
that we are looking into this.

61
00:06:18,625 --> 00:06:21,211
{\an8}Anyone finds out
that we're investigating here

62
00:06:22,004 --> 00:06:23,046
{\an8}it's over.

63
00:06:24,923 --> 00:06:25,966
{\an8}Deal.

64
00:06:26,925 --> 00:06:27,968
{\an8}Deal.

65
00:06:30,304 --> 00:06:31,346
Pound it.

66
00:06:35,142 --> 00:06:36,184
{\an8}Thank you, sir.

67
00:06:51,158 --> 00:06:53,744
- You want a coffee or something?
- I'm good, thanks.

68
00:06:53,994 --> 00:06:55,162
Yeah, it sucks anyway.

69
00:06:56,330 --> 00:06:57,372
I gotta tell you

70
00:06:58,707 --> 00:07:00,876
I'm not thrilled to have my work
checked by the F.B.I.

71
00:07:01,585 --> 00:07:02,878
I'm not thrilled to be here.

72
00:07:04,671 --> 00:07:05,714
Mind if I ask why?

73
00:07:06,131 --> 00:07:09,343
The man you charged with the crime
Zach Jefferson, has taken preliminary steps

74
00:07:09,468 --> 00:07:11,803
to file a civil rights violation
against your department.

75
00:07:12,095 --> 00:07:15,098
- Civil rights violation? For what?
- You tell me.

76
00:07:16,516 --> 00:07:18,018
We caught your man Jefferson

77
00:07:18,518 --> 00:07:20,103
in Nicole Raymond's car

78
00:07:20,687 --> 00:07:22,481
one day after
she was murdered.

79
00:07:23,398 --> 00:07:27,444
The victim's wallet was in the car
as well as jewelry stolen from the house.

80
00:07:27,903 --> 00:07:29,529
Murder scene had signs of a break-in.

81
00:07:30,489 --> 00:07:31,531
Slam dunk.

82
00:07:31,990 --> 00:07:34,034
You look at any other suspects?
The husband, maybe?

83
00:07:34,326 --> 00:07:36,578
Yeah, yeah, 9 times out of 10,
husband did it.

84
00:07:37,037 --> 00:07:39,247
- This is time number 10.
- How can you be so sure?

85
00:07:40,123 --> 00:07:41,166
Check this out.

86
00:07:41,291 --> 00:07:43,377
Aaron Raymond's car
was in the shop that day.

87
00:07:43,502 --> 00:07:46,421
They took Nicole's car.
She dropped him off at work.

88
00:07:47,339 --> 00:07:49,049
They got caught
in a red light camera.

89
00:07:49,758 --> 00:07:51,843
M.E. says based on liver temp

90
00:07:52,219 --> 00:07:55,472
Nicole Raymond was killed
no earlier than 10:00 a.m.

91
00:07:56,556 --> 00:07:58,517
Aaron Raymond got to work
that day at 9:00.

92
00:07:58,892 --> 00:08:00,686
We got confirmation
he never left.

93
00:08:03,730 --> 00:08:05,649
You wanna tell me
how he could be the killer?

94
00:08:17,911 --> 00:08:18,954
What's this about?

95
00:08:19,705 --> 00:08:22,082
We wanted to talk to you
about Nicole Raymond's death.

96
00:08:24,292 --> 00:08:25,335
Why?

97
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:28,338
Zach, the F.B.I. assures me
this is an off-the-record chat.

98
00:08:28,839 --> 00:08:30,424
They just wanna hear our side.

99
00:08:31,675 --> 00:08:32,718
Yeah, right.

100
00:08:36,930 --> 00:08:37,973
Why don't you tell us
what you were doing

101
00:08:38,098 --> 00:08:39,182
before you were arrested?

102
00:08:41,727 --> 00:08:43,478
I was living in a halfway house.

103
00:08:44,021 --> 00:08:45,522
All right? Bagging groceries.

104
00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:50,068
Then I moved in with a friend...
you know, couch surfing.

105
00:08:50,777 --> 00:08:53,238
He got a new girlfriend, she didn't like me
so I had to move out.

106
00:08:56,408 --> 00:08:57,451
You were homeless.

107
00:08:59,244 --> 00:09:00,287
Short-term.

108
00:09:01,079 --> 00:09:02,873
I mean, yeah, I could have moved
back into the halfway house

109
00:09:02,998 --> 00:09:04,708
but them people
be on your case, and...

110
00:09:06,877 --> 00:09:08,420
What were you doing
the day of the arrest?

111
00:09:13,592 --> 00:09:16,762
Look, I found the car. All right?
Keys in it and everything.

112
00:09:16,887 --> 00:09:19,723
Is it really stealing if someone
just leaves something like that?

113
00:09:23,810 --> 00:09:27,439
You've been arrested three times.
Shoplifting, possession.

114
00:09:27,731 --> 00:09:28,774
So?

115
00:09:28,899 --> 00:09:31,610
It's not crazy to think you might try
robbing somebody's house.

116
00:09:32,235 --> 00:09:34,821
Let's just cut right to it.
Did you kill Nicole Raymond?

117
00:09:39,326 --> 00:09:41,286
Man, I never killed anybody.

118
00:09:50,170 --> 00:09:51,213
What?

119
00:09:52,172 --> 00:09:54,216
Well, what do you think?

120
00:09:54,674 --> 00:09:57,010
- You believe him?
- Agents.

121
00:09:59,179 --> 00:10:01,098
It looks bad, but Zach
didn't do this.

122
00:10:01,765 --> 00:10:02,891
He's probably gonna get
the needle.

123
00:10:03,016 --> 00:10:05,769
Obviously, if there's anything
you can do, it'd be appreciated.

124
00:10:07,521 --> 00:10:10,148
If he's telling the truth
then Raymond killed his wife

125
00:10:10,273 --> 00:10:11,942
and then dumped the car
in a bad neighborhood

126
00:10:12,067 --> 00:10:13,777
just hoping somebody
would steal it.

127
00:10:14,111 --> 00:10:16,321
Well, that's not outside
the realm of possibility.

128
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:19,282
Every good plan needs
a little bit of luck.

129
00:10:21,910 --> 00:10:23,620
We should go visit
the crime scene.

130
00:10:38,885 --> 00:10:39,928
Hi, Judy.

131
00:10:40,053 --> 00:10:41,513
My girlfriend and I
are out house hunting

132
00:10:41,638 --> 00:10:44,474
and, uh, we saw a lovely
place on Roosevelt.

133
00:10:44,766 --> 00:10:46,810
Wondering if we could take
a little peek inside.

134
00:10:47,978 --> 00:10:49,229
Yeah, we're out front.

135
00:10:50,021 --> 00:10:51,148
See you soon. Bye.

136
00:10:53,733 --> 00:10:55,026
- On her way.
- Nice.

137
00:10:59,823 --> 00:11:02,033
As you can see, this house
is move-in ready.

138
00:11:04,619 --> 00:11:06,121
There's a lot to love
about this place

139
00:11:06,246 --> 00:11:10,500
but if you ask me, this cozy living area
is a real dream.

140
00:11:10,625 --> 00:11:13,170
- How long has it been on the market?
- A couple months.

141
00:11:13,712 --> 00:11:15,672
Rally? At this price?
What's the catch?

142
00:11:17,215 --> 00:11:18,842
Wait until you
see the master bath.

143
00:11:18,967 --> 00:11:20,802
Is there something
you're not telling us, Judy?

144
00:11:23,388 --> 00:11:24,431
Out with it.

145
00:11:28,185 --> 00:11:30,061
Well, uh, there was
a death here.

146
00:11:30,937 --> 00:11:31,980
A murder.

147
00:11:33,398 --> 00:11:35,317
Shouldn't you have told us
that to begin with?

148
00:11:35,817 --> 00:11:37,277
- Isn't there a law about that?
- Yeah.

149
00:11:37,402 --> 00:11:38,945
I don't know if it's
my responsibility...

150
00:11:39,070 --> 00:11:40,822
You know, I think we're gonna
have to talk about this

151
00:11:41,198 --> 00:11:42,532
if you don't mind, privately.

152
00:11:42,908 --> 00:11:46,077
Oh. Sure. Well, I have some calls to make
so I'll be out in my car.

153
00:11:46,203 --> 00:11:47,787
Just take your time.

154
00:12:00,425 --> 00:12:02,844
Window was open,
screen outside.

155
00:12:03,094 --> 00:12:05,388
The cops think that's how
Jefferson broke into the house.

156
00:12:06,223 --> 00:12:08,350
Through here?
Okay.

157
00:12:13,271 --> 00:12:14,981
Lisbon, take a look at this.

158
00:12:18,026 --> 00:12:19,069
Hi!

159
00:12:20,320 --> 00:12:22,113
Maybe there wasn't
anybody there that day.

160
00:12:22,489 --> 00:12:23,531
Maybe.

161
00:12:24,199 --> 00:12:25,825
What was the weather like
day of the murder?

162
00:12:27,077 --> 00:12:29,871
Crime report says it was...

163
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:31,998
mid 70s, clear skies.

164
00:12:32,249 --> 00:12:34,709
Perfect day for the park.
Which begs a question.

165
00:12:35,126 --> 00:12:37,212
Why would you break in
through that window

166
00:12:38,213 --> 00:12:41,091
when you can break in
around the side through there

167
00:12:41,716 --> 00:12:43,260
hidden from the street?

168
00:12:44,177 --> 00:12:45,971
Now the body was found here

169
00:12:46,388 --> 00:12:48,640
with a half-finished crossword
puzzle and a pen.

170
00:12:48,765 --> 00:12:52,060
So we can assume
that she was sitting... there.

171
00:12:55,814 --> 00:12:57,983
- She would have heard him.
- Yes, she would have heard him.

172
00:12:58,817 --> 00:13:00,443
But if Raymond was the killer...

173
00:13:01,403 --> 00:13:04,072
Very easy to push that screen
out from the inside.

174
00:13:04,239 --> 00:13:06,199
Yeah, but the time of death,
he couldn't have done it.

175
00:13:07,158 --> 00:13:10,787
Sure. That's... That's calculated
from body temperature, right?

176
00:13:11,204 --> 00:13:12,706
Liver temperature, yes.

177
00:13:17,377 --> 00:13:22,048
She had a fan pointed right at her
like it was really hot.

178
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:26,970
But it was Mid 70s.

179
00:13:29,639 --> 00:13:31,599
Perfect day, fan...

180
00:13:33,393 --> 00:13:36,146
- You ever cook a roast?
- Of course.

181
00:13:36,271 --> 00:13:37,397
Yeah. Sorry.

182
00:13:37,897 --> 00:13:41,318
My aunt used to do it this way...
she turned the oven way up high.

183
00:13:42,068 --> 00:13:45,739
You heat it, you put the roast in
and then you turn it off.

184
00:13:48,366 --> 00:13:49,409
So maybe...

185
00:13:51,786 --> 00:13:54,205
He turns the thermostat
all the way up.

186
00:13:57,459 --> 00:13:58,710
Tells her it's broken.

187
00:14:00,587 --> 00:14:03,840
And then he turns the thermostat off
after he kills her.

188
00:14:04,591 --> 00:14:07,177
The room and Nicole
cool off slower

189
00:14:07,927 --> 00:14:10,805
making it seem like she died
later than she actually did.

190
00:14:11,681 --> 00:14:12,724
Exactly.

191
00:14:13,767 --> 00:14:14,809
What about the fan?

192
00:14:16,144 --> 00:14:18,730
"He turned off both the fan
and the thermostat."

193
00:14:23,568 --> 00:14:24,611
"And then he leaves."

194
00:14:25,236 --> 00:14:26,279
Then he leaves.

195
00:14:28,907 --> 00:14:29,949
The front door.

196
00:14:30,658 --> 00:14:32,744
- Looks like it was wiped down.
- For prints.

197
00:14:33,661 --> 00:14:35,330
Raymond doesn't have to
worry about prints.

198
00:14:35,455 --> 00:14:37,332
- It's his house.
- Well, maybe he got blood on it.

199
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:39,125
No. He's too meticulous.

200
00:14:40,126 --> 00:14:42,796
You're right. He wouldn't leave
that mistake in the first place.

201
00:14:43,421 --> 00:14:45,757
Maybe he was wiping off
somebody else's prints.

202
00:14:48,134 --> 00:14:51,221
- Okay. Okay.
- A woman.

203
00:14:51,429 --> 00:14:52,472
It had to have been a woman.

204
00:14:52,597 --> 00:14:54,974
There was a woman with him in the car
for the red light photograph.

205
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:57,060
- After Nicole was already dead.
- Yes.

206
00:14:57,185 --> 00:14:59,145
We need to find out who
that woman was in the car.

207
00:14:59,270 --> 00:15:01,356
It gives him motive.
How do we find her?

208
00:15:01,481 --> 00:15:03,942
Well, I think it's time
I paid our pal Raymond a visit

209
00:15:04,234 --> 00:15:07,237
have a little talk about
"Tactical Solutions to Urban Terrorism."

210
00:15:07,445 --> 00:15:09,030
- I told you.
- Told me what?

211
00:15:09,155 --> 00:15:11,491
You can't quit.
You're having too much fun.

212
00:15:13,159 --> 00:15:14,202
Judy.

213
00:15:14,702 --> 00:15:16,788
I'm sorry. I think we're gonna
have to pass on this one.

214
00:15:16,913 --> 00:15:20,458
- Not enough closet space.
- You heard the woman.

215
00:15:33,276 --> 00:15:34,318
"Yes?"

216
00:15:35,862 --> 00:15:36,904
Colonel Raymond.

217
00:15:38,281 --> 00:15:40,283
The F.B.I. consultant, right?

218
00:15:41,534 --> 00:15:42,952
You here for the seminar?

219
00:15:43,661 --> 00:15:46,289
Oh, that's no way to learn.
I prefer one-on-one.

220
00:15:46,748 --> 00:15:47,790
That's why I'm here.

221
00:15:48,791 --> 00:15:49,959
You need some advice?

222
00:15:50,126 --> 00:15:53,004
Oh, given the way your boys performed
in that game yesterday, I thought

223
00:15:53,296 --> 00:15:55,214
you might be asking
for advice yourself.

224
00:15:56,257 --> 00:15:57,592
Your agents got lucky.

225
00:15:58,217 --> 00:16:01,345
Now I try to teach my men not to
let the other side get lucky like that.

226
00:16:01,888 --> 00:16:04,515
Yeah, I could hear your men
whispering their plans back and forth.

227
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:06,768
Let me tell you something
about plans, Colonel.

228
00:16:07,935 --> 00:16:09,187
They get in the way of instincts.

229
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:10,897
You don't believe in battle plans?

230
00:16:11,314 --> 00:16:15,735
Well, wasn't it Napoleon that once said
"No plan survives the first shot"?

231
00:16:16,652 --> 00:16:19,197
- A student of combat.
- More of people.

232
00:16:21,657 --> 00:16:25,036
Well, Napoleon did all right in his day
but I'd tell him the secret to that is

233
00:16:25,161 --> 00:16:27,538
never let your opponent
fire that first shot.

234
00:16:27,705 --> 00:16:28,748
That's a little unsporting.

235
00:16:29,123 --> 00:16:31,834
Sports are make-believe,
Mr. Jane. War is real.

236
00:16:32,752 --> 00:16:34,629
Maybe the only real thing
we have left.

237
00:16:36,464 --> 00:16:38,257
That's the stupidest thing
I've ever heard.

238
00:16:39,801 --> 00:16:41,052
You're trying to provoke me.

239
00:16:42,136 --> 00:16:43,179
Why?

240
00:16:44,347 --> 00:16:45,389
To see how you react.

241
00:16:48,267 --> 00:16:49,560
Did you learn something?

242
00:16:53,064 --> 00:16:54,107
Yeah.

243
00:16:56,109 --> 00:16:57,151
I think I did.

244
00:16:57,527 --> 00:16:58,569
Thanks for your time.

245
00:17:06,994 --> 00:17:09,705
It's called a rungu.
It's a Masai warrior war club.

246
00:17:11,124 --> 00:17:12,750
It's normal decor for a soldier.

247
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:14,919
Yes, but the African art

248
00:17:15,169 --> 00:17:17,630
doesn't have any analog
in the crime scene photos.

249
00:17:17,880 --> 00:17:18,923
Excuse me?

250
00:17:19,048 --> 00:17:20,341
Well, everything else
in the office...

251
00:17:20,466 --> 00:17:22,552
the books, the photos,
the patriotic art...

252
00:17:22,718 --> 00:17:24,387
all had something similar
in the house

253
00:17:24,512 --> 00:17:26,347
the way it was decorated
the day of the murder.

254
00:17:26,639 --> 00:17:28,850
- But there was no African art.
- No.

255
00:17:30,268 --> 00:17:32,436
So... maybe he had a girlfriend

256
00:17:32,603 --> 00:17:34,814
and maybe she had an interest
in African art.

257
00:17:35,565 --> 00:17:36,607
Should we tail him?

258
00:17:36,941 --> 00:17:39,443
No, he's a soldier in military intelligence.
He can spot a tail.

259
00:17:40,027 --> 00:17:43,030
Most affairs are with people that you meet
in your everyday life.

260
00:17:43,156 --> 00:17:44,991
They're familiar.
Workplace.

261
00:17:45,741 --> 00:17:46,868
So she's a marine.

262
00:17:48,995 --> 00:17:51,247
Well, the Wynn complex has over
a thousand government employees

263
00:17:51,372 --> 00:17:53,708
everything from the N.S.A.
to the post office.

264
00:17:53,875 --> 00:17:54,917
How do we narrow that down?

265
00:17:55,042 --> 00:17:57,044
We need to send someone back
into the Wynn complex

266
00:17:57,170 --> 00:17:58,588
preferably someone
that hasn't been involved

267
00:17:58,713 --> 00:18:01,966
in the training exercises.
That leaves out Lisbon, yourself.

268
00:18:02,091 --> 00:18:03,676
I was there Tuesday
for military training.

269
00:18:04,218 --> 00:18:05,344
Okay. Could you do me a favor?

270
00:18:05,928 --> 00:18:08,806
Next time Wylie steals a glance at you,
could you wave him in?

271
00:18:09,223 --> 00:18:10,266
What?

272
00:18:26,949 --> 00:18:27,992
Stop it.

273
00:18:34,874 --> 00:18:35,917
Hey, I'm from I.T.

274
00:18:36,042 --> 00:18:37,627
- What can I help you with?
- Wrong office.

275
00:18:44,926 --> 00:18:45,968
All right. Thanks.

276
00:18:50,056 --> 00:18:51,599
Hey. From I.T.

277
00:18:58,272 --> 00:19:00,358
Hi, I'm here from I.T.
Thank you.

278
00:19:06,697 --> 00:19:07,740
Come in.

279
00:19:09,992 --> 00:19:11,911
Hey. I'm from I.T.

280
00:19:14,455 --> 00:19:15,831
Is this about Candy Pilots?

281
00:19:17,959 --> 00:19:19,293
'Cause I know you're not supposed to
download games.

282
00:19:19,418 --> 00:19:21,295
I just... I have a lot of down time.

283
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:24,131
What? No.

284
00:19:25,758 --> 00:19:27,510
Okay then. What's up?

285
00:19:28,970 --> 00:19:30,554
I'm here to check
your data stacks.

286
00:19:32,556 --> 00:19:33,599
What's a data stack?

287
00:19:37,061 --> 00:19:39,647
- I asked, what's a data stack?
- Oh, right.

288
00:19:40,564 --> 00:19:41,607
Here, I'll show you.

289
00:19:44,568 --> 00:19:46,487
Denise Sparks, 36.

290
00:19:46,612 --> 00:19:48,948
She works as an information analyst
for the D.O.D.

291
00:19:49,240 --> 00:19:50,658
Could she and Raymond
have crossed paths?

292
00:19:50,950 --> 00:19:54,453
She does a weekly antiterrorism briefing
for top brass, including Raymond.

293
00:19:54,829 --> 00:19:57,540
Love springs up
in the unlikeliest of places.

294
00:19:58,165 --> 00:19:59,208
I found her Facebook page.

295
00:19:59,333 --> 00:20:01,377
She's got lots of photos
of her in Africa.

296
00:20:01,836 --> 00:20:03,379
No pictures of her
and a boyfriend.

297
00:20:03,796 --> 00:20:05,798
Uh, could you put her
next to the red light photo?

298
00:20:10,970 --> 00:20:12,013
That could be her.

299
00:20:13,723 --> 00:20:14,890
Could you engineer a meeting?

300
00:20:15,891 --> 00:20:17,518
Get a read on her,
see if she's our girl?

301
00:20:17,685 --> 00:20:19,437
- Yeah.
- Well, why should I have all the fun?

302
00:20:25,401 --> 00:20:27,820
- What was she like?
- I don't know. She seemed nice.

303
00:20:28,529 --> 00:20:30,031
We're just looking
for impressions.

304
00:20:31,490 --> 00:20:33,743
"She's very clean,
well put together."

305
00:20:35,369 --> 00:20:36,537
"Manicured hands."

306
00:20:36,746 --> 00:20:38,831
"But she chewed the skin
around her nails."

307
00:20:40,750 --> 00:20:41,792
What did she order?

308
00:20:42,710 --> 00:20:44,837
"Coffee, black.
Paid cash."

309
00:20:46,505 --> 00:20:48,549
"She waited until the woman
behind the counter"

310
00:20:48,674 --> 00:20:50,551
"could see her put her tip
in the tip jar."

311
00:20:51,260 --> 00:20:53,679
Interesting. I wonder if I do that.

312
00:20:54,388 --> 00:20:56,140
Four sugars.
It was a lot.

313
00:20:56,265 --> 00:20:57,850
Yeah, that is a lot.
So you talked to her.

314
00:20:57,975 --> 00:20:59,935
- You said, "I like your bag."
- "How'd you know that?"

315
00:21:00,061 --> 00:21:02,063
"Because that's what you think
other women talk about."

316
00:21:03,647 --> 00:21:05,399
Well, it worked. We talked.

317
00:21:05,524 --> 00:21:06,567
Tell me about her.

318
00:21:07,401 --> 00:21:08,652
I don't know. She's reserved.

319
00:21:09,028 --> 00:21:12,573
"It's the kind of quiet where maybe she
could be a raging bitch, or she's just shy."

320
00:21:14,367 --> 00:21:15,451
"And what's your verdict?"

321
00:21:17,078 --> 00:21:19,538
You want me to say if she's a killer
based on spending one minute with her?

322
00:21:19,663 --> 00:21:20,706
Sure.

323
00:21:20,873 --> 00:21:21,916
Well, how do I do that?

324
00:21:22,041 --> 00:21:23,834
Well, your brain's already
processed all the information.

325
00:21:23,959 --> 00:21:25,211
You just have to listen to it.

326
00:21:25,503 --> 00:21:29,131
So, on the count of three,
you're gonna say "yes" or "no."

327
00:21:29,632 --> 00:21:32,426
- 1... 2...
- The answer is yes. I don't need 3.

328
00:21:32,718 --> 00:21:33,761
She could have done it.

329
00:21:34,345 --> 00:21:37,306
Everything about her it just seemed
like she was wearing a mask.

330
00:21:38,182 --> 00:21:39,225
Okay then.

331
00:21:39,934 --> 00:21:41,435
Very nice, Agent Lisbon.

332
00:21:41,560 --> 00:21:42,603
Thank you.

333
00:21:53,614 --> 00:21:57,868
So the theory is
Denise helped Raymond kill his wife

334
00:21:58,327 --> 00:21:59,578
and then she dressed up like him?

335
00:22:00,037 --> 00:22:01,956
Well, Jane thinks they ran
the red light on purpose.

336
00:22:02,081 --> 00:22:04,333
The photograph would give
Raymond an airtight alibi.

337
00:22:04,959 --> 00:22:06,001
Can I see that?

338
00:22:06,836 --> 00:22:07,878
Yeah.

339
00:22:12,633 --> 00:22:14,468
Don't do it fast.
Do it right.

340
00:22:17,513 --> 00:22:19,390
We have nothing
approaching hard evidence.

341
00:22:19,557 --> 00:22:20,975
Yeah, well, that's
where you come in.

342
00:22:23,310 --> 00:22:25,771
This is where the red light
photo was taken.

343
00:22:25,980 --> 00:22:27,982
They dumped the car here,
where Jefferson found it.

344
00:22:28,274 --> 00:22:29,900
It's walking distance
to the Wynn complex.

345
00:22:30,067 --> 00:22:31,318
They walked there
right after the murder?

346
00:22:31,735 --> 00:22:33,863
It's the only way the timing works.
Denise...

347
00:22:34,905 --> 00:22:36,949
Had to change back into
her work clothes at some point.

348
00:22:37,116 --> 00:22:38,701
- Notice she's not driving.
- So?

349
00:22:38,826 --> 00:22:40,828
Jane's theory is she changed
clothes in the car.

350
00:22:40,953 --> 00:22:42,204
Then she had to throw them away.

351
00:22:42,830 --> 00:22:44,582
Maybe she threw them
out the car window.

352
00:22:45,541 --> 00:22:47,877
Or she pulled over and threw it
away in a dumpster.

353
00:22:48,794 --> 00:22:50,546
I don't think they risk
getting out of the car.

354
00:22:52,131 --> 00:22:55,468
Even if Jane's theory is right
that's at least 5 miles.

355
00:22:55,968 --> 00:22:58,220
- I can't search 5 miles.
- You won't have to.

356
00:23:23,913 --> 00:23:24,955
Go.

357
00:23:33,797 --> 00:23:34,840
Don't rush.

358
00:23:35,382 --> 00:23:39,178
I, I mean, she wouldn't have rushed.
And we want it to time out right.

359
00:23:40,721 --> 00:23:41,764
Sure.

360
00:23:55,986 --> 00:23:57,029
Done.

361
00:24:08,707 --> 00:24:10,251
Okay, so now what?

362
00:24:11,210 --> 00:24:12,294
We grid search.

363
00:24:13,295 --> 00:24:15,506
Divide the side of the street
into 1-foot squares.

364
00:24:15,756 --> 00:24:17,299
Examine every square carefully.

365
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:20,511
That's gonna take
a long time.

366
00:24:21,637 --> 00:24:24,557
Keep talking, it'll take even longer.
You heard north, I head south.

367
00:25:09,685 --> 00:25:10,728
Hello?

368
00:25:12,313 --> 00:25:14,982
Hello?!
F.B.I. anyone home?

369
00:25:54,730 --> 00:25:55,773
Vega?

370
00:25:55,898 --> 00:25:56,940
I think I've got something.

371
00:26:15,998 --> 00:26:18,792
Agent Lisbon.
May I have a moment of your time?

372
00:26:19,626 --> 00:26:22,629
- Sure. Have a seat.
- Someplace private.

373
00:26:38,854 --> 00:26:39,897
How can I help you?

374
00:26:40,522 --> 00:26:43,275
I just wanted to know
if you think I murdered my wife.

375
00:26:45,444 --> 00:26:47,905
Well, I certainly
think you did. Hi.

376
00:26:49,823 --> 00:26:50,991
Then arrest me.

377
00:26:54,786 --> 00:26:55,996
That's what I thought.

378
00:26:57,539 --> 00:27:00,042
- What's going on here?
- Just got ambushed.

379
00:27:00,667 --> 00:27:02,169
A Detective Rios called me.

380
00:27:02,419 --> 00:27:03,921
He wanted to know if the F.B.I.
had been touch

381
00:27:04,046 --> 00:27:05,631
about a civil rights case.

382
00:27:06,256 --> 00:27:08,383
Do you know anything
about this little investigation?

383
00:27:13,597 --> 00:27:15,766
Well, that's a damn shame.
I liked you.

384
00:27:16,516 --> 00:27:18,018
The investigation ends now.

385
00:27:19,228 --> 00:27:20,270
Thank you.

386
00:27:21,230 --> 00:27:22,564
You seem like a smart person.

387
00:27:22,940 --> 00:27:24,483
Know when you've
overplayed your hand.

388
00:27:29,279 --> 00:27:31,531
My office. Now.

389
00:27:36,578 --> 00:27:39,373
We've got the fabric
at the lab right now, rush job.

390
00:27:39,665 --> 00:27:42,125
If it holds either Denise
or Raymond's D.N.A.

391
00:27:42,251 --> 00:27:45,128
or some of Nicole's blood
I think we've got a real shot at this.

392
00:27:45,629 --> 00:27:48,257
A piece of fabric
from a homeless encampment.

393
00:27:50,425 --> 00:27:54,263
Even if the lab turns up anything
you don't have it.

394
00:27:55,264 --> 00:27:59,601
Now I think that you and Jane
did some nice work

395
00:28:00,227 --> 00:28:01,812
but not enough
to reopen the case.

396
00:28:02,479 --> 00:28:06,024
No prosecutor is gonna go after this man
without having solid evidence.

397
00:28:06,775 --> 00:28:09,778
We've gotten as close as we can
working off the books.

398
00:28:10,237 --> 00:28:11,571
Maybe if you put us
in front of a judge.

399
00:28:11,697 --> 00:28:15,409
Anything short of a confession
and they will never reopen the case.

400
00:28:16,576 --> 00:28:19,162
I'm sorry. It's over.

401
00:28:25,335 --> 00:28:27,379
When we get the results from the lab
we'll just try again.

402
00:28:29,756 --> 00:28:31,675
Anything short of a confession.

403
00:28:32,384 --> 00:28:33,427
What?

404
00:28:35,679 --> 00:28:37,139
Let's go for the confession.

405
00:29:20,599 --> 00:29:23,477
- Hey.
- What are you doing here?

406
00:29:24,227 --> 00:29:27,689
- Well, I got your message.
- I didn't send you a message.

407
00:29:30,734 --> 00:29:33,070
Morning. Uh, could you
have a seat, please?

408
00:29:33,528 --> 00:29:35,489
I'm sorry. Patrick Jane,
Teresa Lisbon.

409
00:29:41,620 --> 00:29:43,497
He didn't tell you
we're investigating you?

410
00:29:46,208 --> 00:29:47,501
This is outrageous.

411
00:29:48,210 --> 00:29:50,170
- I'm calling Abbott.
- Oh, feel free.

412
00:30:00,972 --> 00:30:03,683
That's embarrassing.
Guess I picked up the wrong phone.

413
00:30:03,975 --> 00:30:05,018
You'll have to talk to him later.

414
00:30:06,978 --> 00:30:08,397
Then I want my lawyer here.

415
00:30:08,522 --> 00:30:10,440
You'll be talking
to your lawyer soon enough.

416
00:30:11,983 --> 00:30:13,026
We'll get to that.

417
00:30:16,238 --> 00:30:17,280
You wanna tell me
what's going on?

418
00:30:17,406 --> 00:30:20,784
Yes. In this briefcase,
I have a report from the crime lab.

419
00:30:20,992 --> 00:30:22,577
I sent something in
to be tested.

420
00:30:22,953 --> 00:30:25,288
When we open it, we will know
if it implicates

421
00:30:25,414 --> 00:30:26,915
one of you, or both of you.

422
00:30:27,749 --> 00:30:30,752
If one of you confesses before
I open the briefcase

423
00:30:31,128 --> 00:30:32,254
we'll cut you a deal.

424
00:30:32,754 --> 00:30:36,591
After the briefcase is open
no deals for anyone.

425
00:30:37,008 --> 00:30:39,970
I'm gonna open the briefcase
in five minutes.

426
00:30:41,763 --> 00:30:43,682
And that five minutes...

427
00:30:46,017 --> 00:30:47,060
Starts now.

428
00:31:08,963 --> 00:31:10,298
You don't have anything on us.

429
00:31:10,590 --> 00:31:12,216
Well, we'll soon see,
won't we?

430
00:31:13,509 --> 00:31:14,552
So exciting.

431
00:31:17,638 --> 00:31:20,016
- I'm walking out of here.
- Go.

432
00:31:22,351 --> 00:31:24,604
- We should go.
- You should stay.

433
00:31:24,979 --> 00:31:27,815
I don't think you understand
how much he hasn't told you.

434
00:31:52,924 --> 00:31:55,510
We'll sit here for five minutes
let them open the briefcase

435
00:31:55,676 --> 00:31:58,429
see that they don't have anything on us
and then we walk out of here.

436
00:31:58,805 --> 00:31:59,847
- Aaron...
- Not a word.

437
00:31:59,972 --> 00:32:01,933
- Definitely in a relationship.
- Yep.

438
00:32:03,100 --> 00:32:04,143
That's not a crime.

439
00:32:04,644 --> 00:32:06,187
- There's no need...
- No... I love him.

440
00:32:09,148 --> 00:32:11,192
- I love you.
- I love you, too

441
00:32:11,359 --> 00:32:12,443
but now's not the time.

442
00:32:13,027 --> 00:32:15,696
- But you know that I would never...
- Of course.

443
00:32:16,531 --> 00:32:17,573
And you know.

444
00:32:27,500 --> 00:32:30,002
If either of you was smart,
this game would be over by now.

445
00:32:33,381 --> 00:32:34,507
Do you ever lay awake at night

446
00:32:34,632 --> 00:32:37,051
wondering what's gonna happen
to you when he gets tired of you

447
00:32:37,844 --> 00:32:39,595
just like he got tired of Nicole?

448
00:32:42,306 --> 00:32:44,392
Colonel, you've carried out
battle plans before.

449
00:32:45,351 --> 00:32:47,353
How hard could
the perfect murder be?

450
00:32:48,145 --> 00:32:51,274
- You did a nice job.
- Very nice. One little mistake.

451
00:32:51,399 --> 00:32:52,525
Was it the thermostat?

452
00:32:53,442 --> 00:32:54,694
Or the wig.

453
00:32:54,819 --> 00:32:57,655
Or was it the clothes
you threw out the window

454
00:32:57,780 --> 00:33:00,575
- on... where was it? 23rd Street.
- 23rd Street.

455
00:33:03,870 --> 00:33:06,998
Time waits for no man...
Or woman.

456
00:33:12,211 --> 00:33:13,421
- This is...
- Stop talking.

457
00:33:14,630 --> 00:33:16,090
- But, Aaron...
- Woman, I'm telling you.

458
00:33:19,010 --> 00:33:20,052
What is it you wanna say?

459
00:33:24,640 --> 00:33:26,058
- "Aaron..."
- Don't be stupid!

460
00:33:45,036 --> 00:33:46,078
All right, I'll talk.

461
00:33:47,413 --> 00:33:49,707
- We did it.
- No, you did it!

462
00:33:49,874 --> 00:33:51,792
No, he's the one who did it!
I didn't do it! He did it!

463
00:33:53,377 --> 00:33:54,420
You...

464
00:34:00,259 --> 00:34:04,180
Nicole said she'd fight the divorce.
She'd take the house, everything.

465
00:34:09,018 --> 00:34:10,061
What right did she have?

466
00:34:15,316 --> 00:34:20,154
Up until the moment she did it
I didn't think it would happen.

467
00:34:27,536 --> 00:34:32,541
He said it would be one bad day
and then a lifetime of happiness.

468
00:34:36,087 --> 00:34:40,299
He told me that she would drag us
both through the mud

469
00:34:41,217 --> 00:34:43,761
that I'd be labeled
a home wrecker.

470
00:34:55,731 --> 00:34:57,108
She said it'd be quick.

471
00:35:08,619 --> 00:35:10,037
She was a different person.

472
00:35:25,803 --> 00:35:26,846
He let me in.

473
00:35:32,101 --> 00:35:34,020
It was so hot.

474
00:35:35,271 --> 00:35:38,315
And when he let me in,
he got the knife off the rack.

475
00:35:40,735 --> 00:35:43,863
He smiled when he did it.

476
00:35:52,747 --> 00:35:53,789
He st...

477
00:35:55,166 --> 00:35:56,208
he stabbed her.

478
00:36:17,813 --> 00:36:19,982
He said something
under his breath when...

479
00:36:20,524 --> 00:36:21,567
"when he killed her."

480
00:36:24,779 --> 00:36:25,821
I don't know what.

481
00:36:33,329 --> 00:36:34,371
"He stabbed her."

482
00:36:36,916 --> 00:36:39,627
"I just...
just stood there..."

483
00:36:44,298 --> 00:36:45,341
"and watched."

484
00:36:53,474 --> 00:36:54,725
She wasn't the same person.

485
00:37:25,131 --> 00:37:27,216
Call Zach Jefferson's attorney.
Give her the good news.

486
00:37:27,341 --> 00:37:28,384
Got it.

487
00:37:28,509 --> 00:37:30,344
Jane, which one was it?

488
00:37:31,011 --> 00:37:32,054
What do you mean?

489
00:37:32,930 --> 00:37:34,723
Who stabbed Nicole?
Was it Raymond or Denise?

490
00:37:35,391 --> 00:37:37,101
- I don't know.
- You don't know?

491
00:37:37,852 --> 00:37:39,186
No. Does it matter?

492
00:37:40,271 --> 00:37:43,524
Well, what...
Well, what's in that?

493
00:37:43,732 --> 00:37:44,942
Did you really get evidence
on one of them?

494
00:37:45,067 --> 00:37:46,235
- Which one was it?
- Good night.

495
00:37:51,115 --> 00:37:52,825
Are you at all curious
about what's in that briefcase?

496
00:37:52,950 --> 00:37:54,618
Haven't stopped thinking
about it for a second.

497
00:37:55,911 --> 00:37:56,954
Should we take a look?

498
00:37:58,455 --> 00:38:00,249
- Ah, I don't know.
- Come on, let's take a look.

499
00:38:01,750 --> 00:38:02,793
Okay.

500
00:38:14,763 --> 00:38:15,806
Meow!

501
00:38:17,600 --> 00:38:18,642
Sorry?

502
00:38:20,311 --> 00:38:23,063
- Okay. Admit it.
- Admit what?

503
00:38:23,314 --> 00:38:24,523
You enjoyed yourself.

504
00:38:24,690 --> 00:38:26,692
You love this job as much as I do.

505
00:38:26,817 --> 00:38:28,652
Well, I love eggs,
but that doesn't mean

506
00:38:28,777 --> 00:38:30,988
I wanna eat nothing but omelets
for the rest of my life.

507
00:38:31,113 --> 00:38:32,489
Fair enough.
What was the alternative?

508
00:38:32,615 --> 00:38:34,533
Pork chops, Cole slaw,
French fries.

509
00:38:34,658 --> 00:38:36,076
What's the alternative for us?

510
00:38:36,202 --> 00:38:37,995
You talk about quitting,
but what are we gonna do?

511
00:38:38,120 --> 00:38:40,289
- What's the plan?
- Well, I don't have one as yet.

512
00:38:40,873 --> 00:38:41,999
Well, that's a problem, isn't it?

513
00:38:42,124 --> 00:38:44,752
Well, if we wait for a plan,
we might never leave.

514
00:38:45,044 --> 00:38:46,962
Maybe we just need to quit
and sit still for a while.

515
00:38:47,087 --> 00:38:48,422
Let life happen.

516
00:38:49,048 --> 00:38:50,758
Let a plan take seed.

517
00:38:51,300 --> 00:38:53,886
Wait for plan seeds to grow?
We'd drive each other crazy.

518
00:38:54,011 --> 00:38:56,639
Maybe. Maybe not.
Maybe we'd be very happy.

519
00:38:57,139 --> 00:38:59,475
- Won't know until we try.
- Aren't you happy now?

520
00:38:59,600 --> 00:39:01,518
Well, I am happy
for the first time

521
00:39:01,644 --> 00:39:04,271
in you know how long,
but I'm scared.

522
00:39:04,897 --> 00:39:06,899
Jane, one of us could get
run over by a bus tomorrow.

523
00:39:07,024 --> 00:39:08,859
Not if we're on a beach
in Polynesia.

524
00:39:08,984 --> 00:39:10,069
Buses can't go on sand.

525
00:39:10,194 --> 00:39:12,321
- You could get eaten by a shark.
- Not if you don't go in the water.

526
00:39:12,446 --> 00:39:14,323
That sounds pretty boring
and wussy, don't you think?

527
00:39:14,448 --> 00:39:17,451
Yeah, they have palm trees
and hammocks and cocktails

528
00:39:17,826 --> 00:39:20,204
and sunsets, pineapples...

529
00:39:20,329 --> 00:39:23,165
And endless boredom, sunburn,
bugs the size of helicopters.

530
00:39:23,290 --> 00:39:25,584
- I've been on vacation.
- Now you're just being negative.

531
00:39:25,709 --> 00:39:27,670
It was an example
of how we could avoid buses.

532
00:39:27,795 --> 00:39:30,005
But we could do
anything we want.

533
00:39:30,506 --> 00:39:32,716
We could learn to sail, buy
a boat, sail around the world.

534
00:39:32,841 --> 00:39:34,093
Yeah, right.

535
00:39:34,301 --> 00:39:38,180
Wh-What? I always wanted to do that.
A lot of people do it.

536
00:39:38,347 --> 00:39:40,224
I mean, we could do it.

537
00:39:40,391 --> 00:39:43,477
As in whales and storms
and pirates? Scurvy?

538
00:39:43,644 --> 00:39:44,812
And besides, I get seasick.

539
00:39:45,104 --> 00:39:46,397
- You do?
- Yeah.

540
00:39:46,522 --> 00:39:48,399
- How badly?
- Pretty bad.

541
00:39:48,941 --> 00:39:50,025
Maybe you'd get over that.

542
00:39:50,234 --> 00:39:51,819
We are not
sailing around the world.

543
00:39:52,569 --> 00:39:53,612
All right.

544
00:39:53,821 --> 00:39:55,698
Beekeeping?
Who doesn't like honey?

545
00:39:55,823 --> 00:39:58,367
Oh, you just come up with
the best ideas. Beekeeping?

546
00:39:58,492 --> 00:40:01,453
Well, I think you'd look very cute
in one of those little beekeeping suits.

547
00:40:01,578 --> 00:40:04,957
How is that romantic?
You...

548
00:40:05,207 --> 00:40:06,250
Yeah?

